WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.200
Throughout this series, we have seen many
impressive applications of artificial intelligence.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.440
These techniques are capable of learning the
piano from the masters of the past, beat formidable

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:21.410
teams in complex games like DOTA 2, perform
well in the game Sonic, The Hedgehog, or help

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:25.470
us revive and impersonate famous actors who
are not with us anymore.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:30.619
However, what is often not spoken about is
how narrow, or how general these AI programs

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:31.619
are.

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:38.890
A narrow AI means an agent that can perform
one task really well, but cannot perform other,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.090
potentially easier tasks.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:45.880
The holy grail of machine learning research
is a general AI that is capable of obtaining

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.610
new knowledge by itself through abstract reasoning.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:55.090
This is similar to how humans learn and is
a critical step in obtaining a general AI.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.740
And to tackle this problem, scientists at
DeepMind created a program that is able to

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.930
generate a large amount of problems that test
abstract reasoning capabilities.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:10.860
They are inspired by human IQ-tests with all
the these questions about sizes, colors and

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.990
progressions.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:16.510
They designed the training process in a way
that the algorithm is given training data

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:21.700
on the progression of colors, but it is never
shown similar progression examples that involve

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.100
object sizes.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.750
The concept is the same, but the visual expression
of the progression is different.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:32.570
A human easily understands the difference,
but teaching abstract reasoning like this

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.340
to a computer sounds almost impossible.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.310
However, now we have a tool that can create
many of these questions and the correct answers

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.310
to them.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.750
And I will note that some of these are not
as easy as many people would expect.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.260
For instance, a vertical number progression
is relatively easy to spot, but have a good

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:55.210
look at these ones...not so immediately apparent,
right?

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:59.990
Going back to being able to generate lots
and lots of data...the black-belt Fellow Scholars

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:02.490
know exactly what this means.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.790
This means that we can train a neural network
to perform this task!

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:11.390
Unfortunately, existing techniques and architectures
perform quite poorly.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:18.560
Despite the fact that we have a ton of training
data, they could only get 22-42% of the answers

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.560
right.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.950
However, these networks are amazing at doing
other things, like writing novels or image

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:29.620
classification, therefore this means that
their generalization capabilities are not

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.780
too great when we go outside their core domain.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.780
This new technique goes by the name "Wild
Relation Network" and is trained in a way

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.450
that encourages reasoning.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:45.010
It is also designed in a way that it not only
outputs a guess for the results, but also

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:51.180
tries to provide a reason for it, which interestingly,
further improved the accuracy of the network.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.010
And what is this accuracy we are talking about?

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:58.910
It finds the correct solution 62.6% of the
time.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:04.020
But it gets better, because this result was
measured in the presence of distractor objects

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.660
like these annoying lines and circles.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:13.350
This is quite confusing, even for humans,
so a result above 60% is quite remarkable.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:18.760
And it gets even better, because if we don't
use these distractions, it is correct 78%

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.069
of the time.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:21.099
Wow!

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:25.849
This is indeed a step towards teaching an
AI how to reason, and as the authors made

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:30.910
this dataset publicly available for everyone,
I expect a reasonable amount of research works

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:36.120
appearing in this area in the near future
- who knows, perhaps even in the next few

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.120
months.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.950
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

